# How many people are alive thanks to synthetic fertilizer?
subtitle: "Sweep across a crowd of a hundred to guess how many owe their food to factory fertilizer, then two hosts unmask the chemist behind it."
tags: ["food","chemistry","history of science"]
format: quiz
thumb: "band \"Air into bread\" note \"1 in 2?\" card blue"
voices: {"a":"gemini:Rasalgethi | an informative quiz host with a light touch; crisp","b":"gemini:Pulcherrima | a forward co-host who says what most people think; natural, never exaggerated","take":"lines"}

## How many people are alive thanks to synthetic fertilizer?
sources:
    - id: owid
      title: How many people does synthetic fertilizer feed?
      authors: Hannah Ritchie (Our World in Data)
      year: 2017
      url: https://ourworldindata.org/how-many-people-does-synthetic-fertilizer-feed
      finding: around half of the global population depends on synthetic nitrogen fertilizer; the Haber-Bosch process has enabled the lives of around 4 billion people today
    - id: owid_chart
      title: World population with and without synthetic nitrogen fertilizers
      authors: Our World in Data, from Erisman et al. (2008) and Rosa & Gabrielli (2023)
      year: 2024
      url: https://ourworldindata.org/grapher/world-population-with-and-without-fertilizer
      finding: '2023: about 3.9 billion of 8.1 billion people (48%) fed by synthetic nitrogen fertilizer'
    - id: erisman
      title: How a century of ammonia synthesis changed the world
      authors: J. W. Erisman, M. A. Sutton, J. Galloway, Z. Klimont, W. Winiwarter
      year: 2008
      finding: synthetic nitrogen fertilizer fed about 48% of the world's population in 2008
    - id: haber
      title: Fritz Haber
      authors: Wikipedia
      year: 2026
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fritz_Haber
      finding: Haber demonstrated ammonia synthesis in 1909; BASF's first plant under Carl Bosch opened in 1913; Nobel Prize in Chemistry 1918; oversaw the first chlorine gas attack, at Ypres in 1915

    population crowd0 fit.h 400 fit.w 920 fit.x 40 fit.y 175 colors.people #8f887c count 100 states.people 100 legend false hidden true
    population crowd cites ["owid_chart"] fit.h 400 fit.w 920 fit.x 40 fit.y 175 colors.fed #87a878 colors.other #8f887c count 100 states.fed 48 states.other 52 order rows legend false labels.fed "fed by fertilizer" labels.other other hidden true

    card title "How many of us does fertilizer feed?"

A: Imagine these hundred people stand for everyone alive. How many of them owe their food to fertilizer made in a factory?
    draw crowd0

    hide crowd0

    ask "Sweep across the people it feeds." on crowd_fed store g default "10"
        right: Close. It feeds nearly half of us.
        wrong: Not quite. Count the people it really feeds.

A: About forty-eight in a hundred: across the world, roughly four billion people.
    text n48 "48 in 100" #5d8a52 cites ["owid_chart"] x 500 y 612 font_size 60

B: Half of us? Fed by a sack of fertilizer?
    highlight crowd_fed

    erase crowd n48

A: By the nitrogen in it. Crops need nitrogen, and the air is mostly nitrogen, but in a form no plant can use.
    icon air size 200 set twemoji x 170 y 470 icon_look picture of "wind face"
    text n78 "78% nitrogen" #2f6b8f x 170 y 320 font_size 40

A: In 1909 the German chemist Fritz Haber found how to pull it out of the air as ammonia, and by 1913 Carl Bosch had it running in a factory.
    arrow a1 #3d3833 from.x 285 from.y 470 to.x 385 to.y 470
    icon hb size 200 set twemoji x 500 y 470 icon_look picture of factory
    text hb_l "Haber–Bosch" #2f6b8f x 500 y 320 font_size 40

B: So half of us eat bread made from thin air.
    arrow a2 #3d3833 from.x 615 from.y 470 to.x 715 to.y 470
    icon bread size 200 set twemoji x 830 y 470 icon_look picture of bread
    text bread_l "4 billion fed" #5d8a52 x 830 y 320 font_size 40

    erase air n78 a1 hb hb_l a2 bread bread_l

A: Pretty much. Haber won the Nobel Prize for it in 1918.
    icon nobel size 220 set twemoji x 280 y 470 icon_look picture of "1st place medal"
    text nobel_l "Nobel, 1918" #2f6b8f cites ["haber"] x 280 y 300 font_size 52

B: Wait, Haber? Isn't he the man behind the poison gas of the First World War?
    icon gas size 220 set twemoji x 720 y 470 icon_look picture of skull
    text gas_l "Ypres gas, 1915" #b5482e cites ["haber"] x 720 y 300 font_size 52

A: He is. He oversaw the first chlorine gas attack, at Ypres in 1915.
    highlight gas_l delivery grave

B: The father of chemical warfare, and the reason billions of people are fed. Quite a legacy.
    highlight nobel_l gas_l

